course: medicine
lesson: 39
title: Sore the next day, stretching and rest days
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: After your first netball training or a long cross-country, your legs may ache so much the stairs feel like a mountain, usually worst one or two days later. That ache is tiny strains in the muscle being repaired, and the muscle comes back stronger.
explain: It is not lactic acid: that clears from the muscles within about an hour. And it is not a score for how hard you tried, because as your body gets used to a sport you ache less even when you work just as hard.
explain: Rest is part of training. Muscles, bones and tendons repair on rest days and during sleep, so playing the same sport every single day, or gaming till midnight before a gala, makes you worse, not better.
explain: Learn the difference: a dull ache in both legs that fades in a few days is soreness, but sharp pain in one spot, swelling or a limp is an injury to tell an adult about.
fact: New or harder exercise causes tiny strains in the muscle; the repair makes it ache, usually worst 1 to 2 days later, and the muscle comes back stronger.
fact: Lactic acid is cleared within about an hour after exercise, so it does not cause next-day soreness.
fact: Soreness mostly comes from doing something new; it is not a measure of a good session, and "no pain, no gain" is wrong.
fact: Normal soreness is a dull ache that eases in a few days; sharp pain in one spot, swelling, or not being able to put weight on a leg means an injury and an adult should know.
fact: Doing the same sport every day without a break can cause overuse injuries; take at least one or two days a week off your main sport.
fact: During deep sleep the body repairs muscles; children of 6 to 12 need about 9 to 12 hours a night, and missing sleep before a big event makes you perform worse.
fact: Stretching after sport helps keep you flexible, but studies show it does very little to stop next-day soreness.
fact: Bouncing in a stretch can pull the muscle; ease in slowly to a feeling of mild tightness, not pain, and hold still for about 20 seconds.
fact: Gentle movement on a rest day, like a walk or an easy swim, often makes stiff muscles feel better than lying still all day.
fact: Children don't need ice baths; very cold water can cause cold shock, a gasp and fast breathing that is dangerous, so never get into icy water, especially alone.
fact: For most children's sport, water is all you need to drink, and sports drinks add a lot of sugar; a proper meal afterwards helps recovery.
exercise: Nia in Cardiff after her first netball training: "My legs are proper aching." What is causing it? | Tiny strains in the muscle being repaired | Lactic acid stuck in her legs for days after the game | A broken bone in each leg | Her muscles shrinking overnight
exercise: Training on Saturday. When is the soreness usually worst? | Sunday or Monday | Saturday night only, then it's gone | The next Saturday | It never comes later than an hour after
exercise: How long does lactic acid take to clear after exercise? | About an hour | About a week or more | About a month | It never clears completely
exercise: A coach shouts, "If you're not sore tomorrow, you didn't try!" What's true? | Soreness is not a measure of a good session | Every good session must hurt the next day | Only players who are sore the next day get better | No soreness means you did nothing all session
exercise: Why do you get less sore as you get used to a sport? | Soreness comes mostly from doing something new | You stop trying as hard once it's easy | Muscles stop working so hard with practice | Lactic acid builds up less in the colder winter months
exercise: Zain in Birmingham: ankle swollen in one spot and he's limping. Is it normal soreness? | No, it may be an injury, tell an adult | Yes, play on, it's just soreness from the game | Yes, it will be gone by tonight | No, it's lactic acid in the ankle
exercise: Kofi plays football every single day and his knees ache all the time. What does he need? | At least one or two rest days a week from football | More football every day to toughen his knees up | Heavier boots for extra support | To train twice a day instead of once a day
exercise: How many hours of sleep do children of 6 to 12 need? | About 9 to 12 | About 4 to 5, like adults | About 6 exactly, every night | About 15 to 18
exercise: Lily games until midnight before her gala, waking at 7. How much less sleep than going to bed at 9? | 3 hours | 2 hours | 7 hours | 10 hours
exercise: Cooper in Melbourne stretches after footy and is still sore the next arvo (afternoon). What's true? | Stretching barely changes soreness but helps flexibility | Stretching always stops soreness completely | Stretching causes the soreness | He stretched for too short a time to stop it working
exercise: Molly bounces in her toe-touch: "It hurts so it's working!" What's right? | Ease in to mild tightness and hold still | Bounce harder and harder until it hurts more | Pain means the stretch is perfect, so push on | Only stretch when your muscles are still cold
exercise: About how long should you hold a gentle stretch? | About 20 seconds | About 1 second each time | About 10 minutes each side | About an hour
exercise: Finn in Galway wants to lie on the couch all day after a hard match. Better? | A gentle walk with the dog | Another full match straight away, to loosen up | Staying perfectly still in bed all day | An ice bath on his own in the garden
exercise: Mason fills the bath with ice like the pros. Why is that a bad idea? | Icy water can cause dangerous cold shock | Ice makes the muscles grow far too fast | Cold water slowly dissolves the muscles | Ice baths are only bad for adults
exercise: Jack plays 4 matches on a hot day with only a can of sports drink. What do most kids need? | Water during and after, then a proper meal | Only sports drinks, never plain tap water | Nothing to drink until home time, to stay light | Five cans of fizzy drink
exercise: After sport, what helps a child recover best? | Water, a proper meal and sleep | An ice bath and no dinner that evening | Gaming late into the night to relax | Sitting out in the hot sun with no drink
test: Arthur: "Can't do the stairs, it's lactic acid, innit." Two days after the race. What's the truth? | It's muscle repair; lactic acid was gone in about an hour | Yes, lactic acid stays in the legs for days | He needs to drink it out of his legs with fizzy pop | It's a sign his legs are broken
test: Coach bellows "No pain, no gain, you soft lot!" What would happen if kids trained to be sore every time? | More aches and injuries, not more progress | They'd all become pros in about a month | Nothing, pain always means gain | Their aches would vanish after one week
test: "My ankle's just sore from footy" — but it's puffy on one side and he can't put weight on it. What do you say? | Tell an adult, that sounds like an injury | Play on, it'll warm up and loosen | Bounce on it a few times to loosen it | It's normal soreness from footy, so ignore it
test: Teammate: "Rest days are for lazy people, bro." What's the fact? | Rest is when the body repairs and gets stronger | Rest makes muscles weaker every single time | Only adults need rest days, not kids | Rest days cause injuries
test: Lily: "Bed's mid, I'll sleep after the gala." What would likely happen? | She'd swim slower, tired in body and brain | She'd swim her best time ever | Nothing, sleep doesn't matter for sport at all | She'd grow faster that night
test: Cooper: "Stretching's useless then, eh?" Is he right? | No, it helps flexibility, just not soreness | Yes, never bother stretching again | Yes, stretching just makes you stiffer the next day | No, it stops all soreness forever
test: Big sis: "Bounce it, bounce it, no pain no gain!" during the toe-touch. Sound advice? | No, bouncing can pull the muscle | Yes, bouncing stretches it the safest way | Yes, the pain proves it's working | No, stretches must always be done cold
test: Finn's mam: "Go on, you eejit (daft lump), walk the dog, it'll do you good." Is she right? | Yes, gentle movement often eases stiff muscles | No, sore muscles need total stillness | No, walking will tear the sore muscles even more | Yes, but only if he runs a 10K with it
test: In a film, a footballer leaps into an ice-cold lake to recover. Real-life rule for kids? | Never get into icy water, especially alone | Ice water is always good for recovery, at any age | Kids should take ice baths daily after sport | Cold water can't hurt you
test: "Water's mid, this has electrolytes." Jack, after one can on a scorching day. What's sensible? | Water is enough for most children's sport | Sports drinks are needed for all kids who sweat | Drinking nothing at all is best in the heat | Fizzy cola replaces water
test: "Crap, my legs are dead, I'll lie on the sofa for a week." What helps soreness fade? | Gentle movement and time, a few days | A week of total stillness on the sofa | More of the same hard training straight away | Ice baths every hour for a day
exam: Identify the principal cause of muscular soreness experienced one to two days after unaccustomed exercise. | Minute strains in the muscle undergoing repair | The retention of lactic acid in the muscle for several days | Fracture of the underlying bones | Deficiency of sports drinks
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning stretching after exercise. | It assists flexibility but scarcely reduces subsequent soreness | It entirely prevents subsequent soreness of the muscles | It is harmful to flexibility in all cases | It is required to remove lactic acid
exam: It is given that a pupil retires at midnight and rises at seven o'clock. Determine the deficit relative to retiring at nine o'clock and rising at the same hour. | Three hours | Two hours | Seven hours | Ten hours
exam: Ascertain which of the following symptoms following sport most strongly indicates an injury rather than ordinary soreness. | Sharp pain and swelling in one spot with a limp | A dull ache in both legs that eases in a few days | Mild stiffness on the morning after training | Tired legs on the evening of a match
